<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Obersalzberg</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Obersalzberg is a mountainside above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berchtesgaden">Berchtesgaden</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavarian-alps">Bavarian Alps</a>. From the 1920s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> stayed there, and after 1933 the area was turned into a fenced compound with his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berghof">Berghof</a>, houses for Nazi leaders, barracks and the Kehlsteinhaus on the summit above. Most of the buildings were bombed in 1945 and later cleared, and a documentation centre on the Nazi period opened there in 1999.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/obersalzberg.jpg" alt="Obersalzberg" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tobi85 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panorama_Kehlsteinstra%C3%9Fe1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Obersalzberg lies about 120 kilometres south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, close to the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. Its name comes from the rock salt of the former Prince-Provostry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berchtesgaden">Berchtesgaden</a>, which ruled it as one of its eight localities named in the land register of 1456. From 1517 the Petersberg gallery, the first of the Berchtesgaden salt mines, was built there and became the economic base of the provostry. The area passed to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> in 1810, Salzberg became a Bavarian municipality in 1817, and it was joined to Berchtesgaden only in 1972.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1877 Mauritia Mayer, a pioneer of Alpine tourism, opened the Pension Moritz boarding house, and visitors such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-brahms">Johannes Brahms</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clara-schumann">Clara Schumann</a> came there. In 1923 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> visited the party member Dietrich Eckart at the boarding house, and after his release from prison in 1925 he dictated Part Two of Mein Kampf in a cabin on the premises. By 1928 he was renting Haus Wachenfeld nearby, which he bought in 1933 and rebuilt by 1936 into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berghof">Berghof</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermann-goring">Hermann Göring</a>, Martin Bormann and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-speer">Albert Speer</a> acquired houses around it. By 1935-36 Bormann had bought out or evicted all the residents, and the area became a guarded retreat with an SS barracks, a cinema and a school, divided into three security zones. Hitler spent much of August 1939 at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berghof">Berghof</a> planning the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>; his last known visit was on 14 July 1944. An Allied air raid of 25 April 1945 badly damaged the buildings except the Kehlsteinhaus, and on 4 May retreating SS troops set fire to the Berghof on Hitler's earlier orders. The U.S. 3rd Infantry Division arrived hours later, and the house was looted.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In 1952 the Bavarian government had the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berghof">Berghof</a> and more than 50 Nazi-era buildings demolished so that they would not become a shrine. The Dokumentationszentrum Obersalzberg, run by the Institute of Contemporary History, describes the site and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazism">National Socialism</a> and lets visitors tour the bunker complex. The area returned to the Bavarian state in 1995, and the Kehlsteinhaus opens seasonally as a restaurant.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Obersalzberg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 20th century Hitler's Berghof stood on the <b>Obersalzberg</b> above the town.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-provostry-of-berchtesgaden">Prince-Provostry of Berchtesgaden</a></li></ul></section>
